\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For deterministic machines with one writable tape and head, finitely many read-only input heads moving by unit steps from fixed origins, cap-independent symbol functions and the prescribed local-access condition, can the finite-control and halting outcome by binary time cap T be simulated in $\widetilde O(T^{2/5})$ workspace bits, excluding read-only input storage, with unrestricted simulation time and no requirement to materialize the final tape?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For deterministic machines with one writable tape and head, finitely many read-only input heads moving by unit steps from fixed origins, cap-independent symbol functions and the prescribed local-access condition, can the finite-control and halting outcome by binary time cap T be simulated in $\widetilde O(T^{2/5})$ workspace bits, excluding read-only input storage, with unrestricted simulation time and no requirement to materialize the final tape?

\subsection{Sources}
